        "description": "<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/1.7598215,1753932801000/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C222%2C4240%2C2385%29%3BResize%3D%28620%29' alt='A balding man wearing army fatigues and glasses sits on a sofa in front of Canadian flags and speaks while gesturing with his hands.' width='620' height='349' title='The country&apos;s top army commander, Lt.-Gen. Mike Wright, acknowledged in an interview with CBC News on Wednesday, July 30, 2025, that the credibility and reputation of the Canadian Army is once again being questioned following investigations into alleged extremist activities and revelations of inappropriate social media posts.'/><p>Canada's army is preparing a sweeping reorganization just as the National Defence Department moves to finalize its mobilization plan. Senior commanders warn the current force is ill suited to a more dangerous world, with proposals under study to expand reserves and generate hundreds of thousands of additional personnel in a crisis.\n</p>",
